The Pico side already does this. emote1_ble_faces.py listens on the Nordic UART RX characteristic, and your app only needs to send one line of text per face. You can ignore face_to_ble.py.

What the app has to send

Write this to the RX characteristic (6E400002-B5A3-F393-E0A9-E50E24DCCA9E), ending with a newline:

FACEDEF:<name>|<W>x<H>|<hex>|<optional caption>
Name: letters, numbers, _ or -, up to 16 characters.
Size: 64x64 is typical, but any width up to 128 and height up to 64 works. The height must be a multiple of 8.
Hex: the pixel data, 2 hex characters per byte. A 64x64 face is 512 bytes, so 1024 hex characters.
Chunking: split the line into writes of 20 bytes, or your negotiated MTU minus 3. The Pico reassembles everything up to the newline.

How to pack the pixels

The display wants 8 vertical pixels per byte:

bytes = array of (W * H / 8) zeros
for y in 0..H-1:
    for x in 0..W-1:
        if pixel(x, y) is lit:
            bytes[(y // 8) * W + x] |= 1 << (y % 8)
hex = bytes as lowercase hex string

Lit means white on the OLED, so black pixels stay 0.

Using the face afterward

FACE:<name> shows it for 3 seconds.
BASE:<name> sets it as the idle face.
FACEDEF:normal|... replaces the built-in idle face directly, and the other built-in faces work the same way.

Checking that it arrived

The Thonny shell prints [Face] Stored 'name' (64x64). If your app subscribes to notifications on the TX characteristic (6E400003-...), it also gets FACEDEF:OK:name or FACEDEF:ERR:<reason>. The reasons are format, name, size, hex, length and full.

If your app already sends faces in a different format, such as raw bytes, base64, a row-by-row bitmap or ASCII text, tell me what it sends and I’ll change the Pico’s parser to accept that instead of changing the app.
